1563		\leanok
1564		\uses{def:ith-restricted-line, def:line-representative, def:combine-map}
1565		Put $n=2m+2$. The directly indexed extended line-point law samples
1566		$i\in\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ and $u,v\in\F_q^n$ independently and uniformly,
1567		and chooses the axis or diagonal kind with equal probabilities.
1568		For the axis kind set $w=e_i$; for the diagonal kind set $w_j=0$ for
1569		$j<i$ and $w_j=v_j$ for $j\ge i$. The line has base $L_w^{\mathrm{lnf}}(u)$
1570		and direction $w$, retaining its kind and coordinate index; the sampled
1571		point is $u$. Zero diagonal directions are included.
1572	
1573		A directly indexed sub-line law $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ is a probability law
1574		on triples $(\ell,\ell_X,\ell_Z)$ with the following properties.
1575		Its extended-line marginal is the line marginal just defined.
1576		Every supported triple admits affine maps $t\mapsto a_W+b_Wt$ such that
1577		\[
1578			\pi_W(\ell(t))=\ell_W(a_W+b_Wt),\qquad W\in\{X,Z\},
1579		\]
1580		where $\ell(t)$ denotes base plus parameter times direction and the
1581		field identifications are understood. Thus each extended point projects
1582		onto its two source lines, with affine scalar coordinates $\alpha(t)$
1583		and $\beta(t)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:combine-map}. An axis-parallel
1584		extended line has axis-parallel source lines.
1585	
1586		Finally there is one probability law on
1587		$(\mathrm v,i,j)\in\{\mathrm{ALine},\mathrm{DLine}\}
1588		\times\{0,\dots,m-1\}^2$ with the following two mixture properties.
1589		Sample a triple from $D_{\mathrm{dir}}$ and an independent uniform
1590		parameter $t$ on $\ell$. The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_X(\ell(t)))$
1591		is the mixture obtained by sampling $(\ell_X,x)$ from
1592		$D_{\mathrm v,i}$ and independently $\ell_Z$ from the line marginal of
1593		$D_{\mathrm v,j}$, and retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,x)$.
1594		The law of $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,\pi_Z(\ell(t)))$ is the mixture obtained
1595		by retaining $(\ell_X,\ell_Z,z)$ from the corresponding independent
1596		samples of $\ell_X$ and $(\ell_Z,z)$. These are separate one-point
1597		marginal identities, with the same mixture weights; they impose no
1598		joint conditional independence of the two projected points.
1599		Identification with the source law $D$ is a separate obligation;
1600		see \cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
1601	\end{definition}
